Objective
The objective of this lab was to understand and exploit NTLM authentication mechanisms within a Windows environment. The focus was on analyzing authentication flows, capturing and accessing password hashes, and leveraging tools to extract credentials from memory.
Step 1: Understanding NTLM Authentication
I began by reviewing how NTLM authentication works within a Windows environment, focusing on the challenge-response mechanism used for verifying user credentials.
During this process, I observed:
NTLM authentication relies on hashed credentials instead of plaintext passwords
Authentication exchanges can be intercepted or analyzed
This highlighted how NTLM can be targeted for credential access and exploitation.
Step 2: Accessing Authentication Hashes
Next, I worked on accessing NTLM hashes generated during authentication.
Through this process, I was able to:
Identify where hashes are stored or processed
Capture or access authentication-related data
This demonstrated how attackers can obtain hashed credentials without directly knowing the plaintext password.
Step 3: Credential Extraction with Mimikatz
With access to the system, I utilized Mimikatz to extract credentials from memory.
This allowed:
Retrieval of NTLM password hashes
Access to credential material stored in memory
This step showed how powerful post-exploitation tools can be used to extract sensitive authentication data from compromised systems.
Step 4: Analyzing Retrieved Hashes
After extracting the hashes, I analyzed them for potential use in further attacks.
This included:
Identifying valid user accounts
Preparing hashes for potential reuse or cracking
This demonstrated how attackers can leverage harvested hashes for lateral movement or privilege escalation.
Step 5: Impact Analysis
Combining all findings, the environment was vulnerable due to weaknesses in credential handling and exposure:
NTLM authentication susceptible to hash access
Credentials stored in memory
Lack of protections against credential dumping
An attacker could leverage these issues to:
Extract and reuse authentication hashes
Perform pass-the-hash attacks
Escalate privileges within the environment
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this lab, I gained hands-on experience working with NTLM authentication and credential extraction techniques. This exercise reinforced the importance of securing authentication mechanisms and protecting credentials in memory.
It also highlighted how attackers can leverage hashed credentials to move laterally and compromise additional systems without needing plaintext passwords.
